From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] Migrate 0417-speed-09-invalidation-filter.patch

Source: 0417-speed-09-invalidation-filter.patch
GPU-write invalidations of pages they never held

Port of zortos293/Lichii PR 250 (thibaut, 2e5d6d6ebd), behind a switch. The GPU's own cached
guest writes (WriteBlockCached: constant buffer data, every draw) are invalidated in the texture,
buffer, query and pipeline caches at the next draw (FlushCaching -> InnerInvalidation). The
texture, query and shader caches only visit the objects registered on those pages, and constant
buffer memory holds none: each call still took the cache's lock and walked its page tables, about
88k times a second in Ocarina of Time on the source's machine (+3.4%, 52.0 -> 53.6 fps, RTX 4090).

EDEN_NX2_INVALIDATION_FILTER=1: each of the three keeps a PageFilter, one bit per 4 KiB device
page it ever registered an object on (images, cached queries, shaders), never cleared. A write
whose pages have no bit set cannot concern that cache, so InnerInvalidation skips it. The buffer
cache is still always told. Changed from the source: the filter's words (2 MiB per cache) are
allocated only with the switch; without it Mark does nothing and Any answers true, so the four
caches are told as before and the cost is one pointer test per call.

On the console this runs on the back thread (engine methods), inside the 60% of its time that is
not idle (run108). Not measured there. Clang syntax check clean on vk_rasterizer.cpp,
shader_cache.cpp, vk_texture_cache_base.cpp and vk_query_cache.cpp.
